      "notes": "The verdict is internally consistent and well-reasoned for O12. The additionality concern is correctly flagged in both the rationale and biggest_unknown. The IFS claim (E32) is accurately characterised \u2014 the quote says the aid cut 'primarily facilitates smaller cuts to other unprotected departments rather than directly funding defence increases,' and the claim renders this faithfully without overstating it. Tier assignments are appropriate: baseline spending figures are measurable, projections are labelled projected, policy text is stated. The direction 'improves' is defensible on narrow O12 grounds \u2014 reduced discretionary spending reduces the deficit regardless of additionality concerns, and the rationale explicitly tempers confidence on that basis. No party-identity language or motive attribution detected. No false balance issue \u2014 the verdict does not reach mixed/too-uncertain. No magnitude_timing violation \u2014 direction is not too-uncertain, and magnitude/time_horizon are not n/a. One minor note: the E32 quote refers specifically to the current government's aid cuts in the context of defence spending, not to Reform UK's proposed additional cut; the claim applies it to the Reform proposal by analogy. This is a reasonable inference but slightly extrapolates beyond the quote. This is a warning-level concern at most and does not block publication."
